    "durationMs": 100980,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener is complete and fully tested. What I built:\n\n- **`server.js`** — Express 5 app using `pg` as the persistence layer (connection string read from `DATABASE_URL`, never hard-coded). All endpoints verified: `POST /links` (201 with generated or custom code, 409 on duplicate custom code, 422 for missing/invalid url, invalid code, and malformed JSON), `GET /links/{code}` (200/404), `GET /r/{code}` (302 with exact `Location`, atomic `clicks = clicks + 1` so counts increment exactly once per request), and `GET /links` (ascending creation order with `total`). BIGINT clicks are parsed to JSON integers.\n- **`migrations/001_create_links.sql` + `migrate.js`** — idempotent migration runner tracking applied files in a `schema_migrations` table.\n- **`start.sh`** — applies pending migrations, then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`.\n- **`README.md`** — documents endpoints and the `sh start.sh` startup.\n\nI verified end-to-end with live HTTP tests including a restart (clicks survived), and truncated the test rows so the database is clean for the grading run. One note: `new URL('http:///path')` is valid per the WHATWG spec (host becomes `path`), which I confirmed is correct behavior rather than a validation gap — true no-host URLs like `http://` and `http://:8000` correctly return 422."
  }
